Acceptance criteria:
  Teach from no assumed programming or game-engine knowledge. Sequence
    prerequisites, runnable exercises, changed-case retries, diagnostics and
    meaningful artifacts; do not deliver only a list of topics or links.
  Godot/GDScript is the main path. Include both2D and3D foundations and explain
    which advanced specializations are optional rather than promising every
    possible studio discipline as a mandatory first-project requirement.
  The capstone is a concrete, original, bounded multiplayer reference design,
    not a renamed engine tutorial. Specify story, game loop, controls, rules,
    content, failure/recovery, technical ownership, milestones and acceptance.
    Detailed creative content belongs in the intended library/source; mirror
    only this generic workstream into issues or shared continuity.
  Provide reproducible multiplayer/network failure scenarios, observable
    self-grading criteria and critical completion gates. A specification is
    not a built/tested Godot game, actual playtest or automatically awarded skill.
  New and compatible older workspaces gain only the missing canonical unstarted
    Planned mission. Preserve existing focus, modes, dates, records and explicit
    valid game state; reject malformed explicit state rather than resetting it.
  Planned means available curriculum awaiting deliberate activation, not an
    undocumented or permanently disabled mission. Browsing gives no completion,
    daily-plan, readiness, recall, evidence or discovery credit.
  Keep the enlarged type, accepted minimalist UI, themes, meaningful3D and
    frozen source editions. Use existing library/disclosure/navigation patterns.
  Use disposable examples, not the owner's browser/profile or private backup.
    Retain both user previews; no unapproved engine install, network exposure,
    paid service, commit, push, deployment or game publication.

